  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an array AAA of size 2N2N2N, where every number from 111 to NNN occurs **exactly twice**.\n\nConsider choosing a subsequence of positions t=(t1,t2,…,tM)t = (t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_M)t=(t1​,t2​,…,tM​) with 1≤t1&lt;t2&lt;⋯&lt;tM≤2N1 \\le t_1 &lt; t_2 &lt; \\cdots &lt; t_M \\le 2N1≤t1​&lt;t2​&lt;⋯&lt;tM​≤2N.\n\nThis subsequence is called **valid** if for every starting point jjj with 1≤j≤M−N+11 \\le j \\le M - N + 11≤j≤M−N+1, the values\n\nAtj,Atj+1,…,Atj+N−1A_{t_j}, A_{t_{j+1}}, \\ldots, A_{t_{j+N-1}}Atj​​,Atj+1​​,…,Atj+N−1​​ are all **mutually different**.\n\nThat is, within every sliding window of length NNN inside the chosen subsequence, no value appears more than once.\nYour task is to determine the **largest possible size** of such a valid subsequence.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains an integer TTT — the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines:\n\nThe first line contains single integer NNN, where NNN is the number of distinct values.\nThe second line contains 2N2N2N integers A1,A2,…,A2NA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_{2N}A1​,A2​,…,A2N​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, print one integer - the **maximum** possible length of a valid subsequence.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\le T \\le 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\le N \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Ai≤N1 \\le A_i \\le N1≤Ai​≤N for all iii",
      "Each integer from 111 to NNN occurs exactly twice in the array.",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n1\n1 1\n3\n1 1 2 2 3 3\n3\n1 1 2 3 2 3\n3\n1 1 2 3 3 2",
        "output": "2\n3\n3\n3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nFor Test Case 1:\n\nWindow size = 1, and single elements are always distinct. We can take both.\nHence answer is 2.\n\nFor Test Case 2:\n\nYou can pick one of each: {1,2,3}.\nAny extra pick repeats a number inside a window of size 3.\nHence, answer is 3."
      }
    ]
